@Tutorial(time: 30) {
    @Intro(title: "Build the Aquarium Scene") {
        Create an iOS app project, connect a Reality Composer Pro package,
        and show a scene with a seahorse model in SwiftUI's RealityView.

        Along the way you see the whole path 3D content takes: from Reality
        Composer Pro, through `AquariumContent`, onto the app's screen.

        @Image(source: "01-intro", alt: "A seahorse model placed in the Reality Composer Pro viewport")
    }

    @Section(title: "Prepare the Project and the RCP Package") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Create an iOS app project and connect a Reality Composer Pro
            package to hold the 3D content.

            It's easy to think of an RCP package as just a folder of assets,
            but it is actually a **Swift Package** that holds 3D content
            together with Swift code for that content. Custom components you
            write in later chapters also go inside this package, not the app
            project.

            Unlike the visionOS app template, the iOS app template doesn't
            create an RCP package for you. So you create the `AquariumContent`
            package yourself in Reality Composer Pro and connect it so the
            `RoomAquarium` app in Xcode can use it.

            @Image(source: "01-section1", alt: "The AquariumContent package added to the Xcode Project Navigator")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In Xcode, choose **File > New > Project**. Pick the **App**
                template under the **iOS** tab at the top, then click
                **Next**.

                Name the project `RoomAquarium` and set Interface to
                **SwiftUI**.

                @Image(source: "01-s1-step1", alt: "Creating the RoomAquarium project from the iOS App template in Xcode")
            }

            @Step {
                In the project settings, go to **TARGETS > RoomAquarium >
                General**. Check that **Minimum Deployments** is **iOS 18.0**
                or later.

                Features this tutorial uses, such as RealityView, need iOS 18
                or later on iPhone and iPad.

                @Image(source: "01-s1-step2", alt: "Setting Minimum Deployments of the RoomAquarium target to iOS 18.0 or later")
            }

            @Step {
                From the menu bar, choose **Xcode > Open Developer Tool >
                Reality Composer Pro**.

                Reality Composer Pro (RCP from here on) ships with Xcode, but
                it opens as a separate app window.

                @Image(source: "01-s1-step3", alt: "Launching Reality Composer Pro from the Xcode menu")
            }

            @Step {
                In RCP, choose **File > New** to create a new project. Name it
                `AquariumContent`. For the location, pick the project root
                folder that contains RoomAquarium.xcodeproj — not the inner
                RoomAquarium folder with the app's source, but the spot next
                to it.

                Creating an RCP project produces a Swift Package that contains
                an `.rkassets` folder and Swift code. Everything you build in
                RCP from now on — scenes and custom component code — is saved
                in this package.

                > Important: The `AquariumContent` module name and the
                > `aquariumContentBundle` bundle constant are generated from
                > the package name. Use the same names as the tutorial.

                @Image(source: "01-s1-step4", alt: "Creating the AquariumContent package inside the RoomAquarium project folder")
            }

            @Step {
                Go back to Xcode. In the Project Navigator, right-click the
                top-level `RoomAquarium` project and choose **Add Files to
                "RoomAquarium"**.

                Select the `AquariumContent` folder you just created. It is
                already in the right place at the project root, so add it as a
                reference without copying or moving it. If your Xcode version
                shows file-adding options, don't choose **Copy items if
                needed** or **Move files to destination**. When it's added,
                check that `AquariumContent` appears in the Project Navigator
                with a brown package icon.

                @Image(source: "01-s1-step5", alt: "Adding the AquariumContent package to the RoomAquarium project in the Project Navigator")
            }

            @Step {
                Link the `AquariumContent` package product to the app target.

                In the project settings, go to **TARGETS > RoomAquarium >
                General**. Click the **+** button under **Frameworks,
                Libraries, and Embedded Content**, choose `AquariumContent`
                under **Workspace > AquariumContent**, and click **Add**. This
                lets the `RoomAquarium` app use the package's Swift code and
                its 3D content bundle.

                @Image(source: "01-s1-step6", alt: "Adding the AquariumContent package to the RoomAquarium app target")
            }

            @Step {
                Check that the app can use the package.

                Open one of the app's Swift files and type
                `import AquariumContent`. Check that the `AquariumContent`
                module shows up in autocomplete, then press **Cmd+B** to
                build. If the import line has no error and Xcode shows
                **Build Succeeded** at the top, the package is connected.

                > Important: If it doesn't connect, check the following.
                >
                > 1. `AquariumContent` is in the Project Navigator with a
                >    brown Swift Package icon.
                > 2. `AquariumContent` is listed under the app target's
                >    **Frameworks, Libraries, and Embedded Content**.
                > 3. The `Package.swift` of the `AquariumContent` package
                >    defines a library product and a target with that name.
                > 4. The package module name and `import AquariumContent`
                >    match exactly, including capitalization.

                @Image(source: "01-s1-step7", alt: "Xcode after importing the AquariumContent module and building successfully")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Prepare the Aquarium Content in RCP") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Prepare the 3D model and audio asset for the aquarium in RCP.

            Add `seahorse.usdz` from Apple's AR Quick Look Gallery and the
            Whale Cry audio asset from the RCP Asset Library to the package.
            Then place the seahorse in the scene as a single entity, and set
            up the components it needs to receive user input later.

            You don't build any interaction yet. This section focuses on
            placing the model in the scene and preparing the structure that
            interaction will need.

            @Image(source: "01-section2", alt: "The seahorse scene and the Project Browser in Reality Composer Pro")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Find the seahorse model in Apple Developer's
                [AR Quick Look Gallery](https://developer.apple.com/augmented-reality/quick-look/)
                and download it.

                Back in RCP, open the **Project Browser** at the bottom of the
                window and drag the downloaded seahorse model straight into
                it. You can also choose **File > Import File** from the menu
                bar. For the whale sound, click the **+** button at the top of
                RCP to open the Asset Library, search for Whale Cry, and add
                it. When the import finishes, you'll see `seahorse.usdz` and
                `WhaleCry.usdz` in the Project Browser.

                @Image(source: "01-s2-step1", alt: "The seahorse and Whale Cry assets added to the Project Browser")
            }

            @Step {
                Double-click `Scene` in the Project Browser to open it. Check
                that `Root` appears in the Scene Hierarchy on the left and an
                empty scene is open in the viewport in the middle.

                Drag `seahorse.usdz` from the Project Browser onto `Root` in
                the Scene Hierarchy. When a new entity appears under `Root`,
                an instance of the seahorse model has been added to the scene.
                Select the new entity, rename it `Seahorse`, and adjust
                Position and Scale under **Transform** in the inspector on the
                right so the seahorse is easy to see in the viewport.

                @Image(source: "01-s2-step2", alt: "The seahorse entity placed in the RCP scene and renamed Seahorse")
            }

            @Step {
                Check in the hierarchy panel that `Seahorse` is under `Root`,
                then save with **Cmd+S**.

                Saving in RCP updates the `.rkassets` contents inside the
                `AquariumContent` package right away. There's no separate
                export step before the app can use it.

                @Image(source: "01-s2-step4", alt: "The saved scene with Seahorse placed under Root")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Show the Scene in a RealityView") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Bring the scene you prepared in RCP into the SwiftUI app.

            SwiftUI's **RealityView** is the view that shows RealityKit 3D
            content on screen. You load the scene saved in the
            `AquariumContent` package, add it to a RealityView, and check
            that the seahorse appears in the running app.

            This completes the whole path that carries 3D content from
            `AquariumContent` to SwiftUI's RealityView.

            @Image(source: "01-section3", alt: "The seahorse scene shown in a RealityView")
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add a new Swift file, `AquariumView.swift`, to the Xcode
                project. Import `SwiftUI`, `RealityKit`, and `AquariumContent`,
                and write a RealityView in the body.

                The `content` in the RealityView closure is where the
                RealityKit content shown on screen lives. Next, you add the
                scene you made in RCP here.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "01-code-02.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Load the `Scene` you made in RCP with `Entity(named:in:)` and
                add it to `content`.

                Reading 3D content can take time, so you use `await`.
                `"Scene"` is the scene's name inside `AquariumContent.rkassets`,
                and `aquariumContentBundle` is the bundle constant the package
                generates.

                > Important: AquariumContent is a module name, so it starts
                > with a capital letter, as in import AquariumContent.
                > aquariumContentBundle, on the other hand, is a Swift
                > constant that points to the bundle, so it starts with a
                > lowercase letter.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "01-code-03.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Add `.realityViewCameraControls(.orbit)` to the RealityView.

                Now you can drag the screen to rotate the scene and pinch to
                zoom in and out. If you need to, move the scene so the
                seahorse sits well in the camera's view.

                @Code(name: "AquariumView.swift", file: "01-code-04.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Open RoomAquariumApp.swift, the app's entry point, and change
                the default ContentView() in the WindowGroup to the new
                AquariumView().

                When you build and run the app and the seahorse appears in the
                RealityView inside AquariumView, the scene in the
                AquariumContent package is correctly connected all the way to
                the app.

                @Code(name: "RoomAquariumApp.swift", file: "01-code-05.swift") {
                    @Image(source: "01-s3-result", alt: "The seahorse shown in the app's RealityView")
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
